  "slug": "genome-gene-a176",
  "summary": "Single-cell eQTL and caQTL atlases across immune cell types now connect common and rare variants to regulatory programs and disease risk.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the move from locus-level association toward cell-type-resolved regulatory interpretation. Single-cell resources feature prominently among the published work: TenK10K maps common and rare variant effects on expression across 28 immune cell types from over 5 million cells, a separate single-cell ATAC study links caQTLs from 3.5M PBMCs to eQTLs and GWAS disease signals, and tandem repeat variation is shown to shape cell type-specific expression. Mendelian randomization is applied at cell-type resolution in scMRdb and in several disease-specific studies. On the statistical side, happler fine-mapping models cis haplotype effects to tag causal variants, including ungenotyped and structural ones. Regulatory network inference (SPARK) and transcription factor programs (TFXcan) extend this chain toward mechanism. The novelty lies in the joint use of haplotype-aware fine-mapping, single-cell molecular QTL maps, and cell-stratified causal inference rather than any single method.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-30"
}
